\title{\textbf{Beyond Chain-of-Thought: Test-Time Compute Scaling for Deliberative Large Language Models}}
\author{Anonymous}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Scaling the \emph{training} compute of large language models (LLMs) has yielded remarkable generality, but many tasks still require adaptive, structured \emph{test-time} computation to support extended deliberation, search, and self-evaluation.
Existing practices---such as chain-of-thought (CoT), parallel self-consistency sampling, and simple tree-of-thought search---offer important first steps but leave open a systematic theory of how and when to allocate additional inference-time computation and what algorithms achieve the best return on cognitive effort. 
This paper proposes a unifying formalization of \emph{budgeted deliberation}, defines the \emph{Budget--Performance Frontier} for test-time compute, and introduces a family of theoretically grounded algorithms that go substantially beyond linear CoT and naive parallelization. 
Drawing on resource-rational analysis, decision theory, submodular optimization, bandit indices, risk-sensitive control, and insights from cognitive psychology and neuroscience, we develop: Index-Guided Deliberation across competing reasoning threads; Risk-Sensitive Monte Carlo Tree-of-Thought; Abduction--Deduction--Refutation loops driven by information gain; Counterfactual Self-Consistency as message passing; Market-based Decompose--Recompose allocation; Branch-and-Bound with LLM-admissible heuristics; Annealed Populations of Thoughts; and Deliberative External Memory with ephemeral indexing. 
We provide conditions under which these methods enjoy anytime behavior, approximate optimality, or risk control, and we propose evaluation criteria that quantify the efficiency of test-time compute. In addition, we present a synthetic evaluation that illustrates BPF estimation, marginal value-of-compute, ablations, effect-size reporting, and multiple-testing corrections. 
\end{abstract}

\vspace{-0.5em}
\section{Introduction}

Recent LLMs demonstrate impressive problem-solving abilities, especially when prompted to ``think aloud'' \cite{wei2022cot} or when multiple samples are reconciled via self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency}.
However, naive increases in the number of tokens or parallel samples often yield diminishing returns, lack principled stopping rules, and can entrench spurious reasoning.
We ask: \emph{How should inference-time computation be allocated to maximize expected solution quality per unit of cognitive effort?}
And: \emph{What algorithms move us beyond linear chain construction or blind parallelism toward structured, risk-aware deliberation?}

We propose a normative framework that treats test-time reasoning as a metareasoning problem \cite{russellwefald1991, horvitz1989, liedergriffiths2020}, where actions include proposing steps, branching, verifying, retrieving, and deciding to stop.
Within this framework we design complex, yet practical, test-time compute algorithms whose decision rules are derived from principles of expected value of computation (EVC), submodularity, bandit indices \cite{gittins1979,whittle1988restless}, and risk-sensitive search \cite{howard1972risk,tamar2015pgcrm}.
To ground these algorithms psychologically and neurally, we connect to dual-process theories \cite{kahneman2011}, working memory and cognitive control \cite{baddeley1992workingmemory,gershmandaw2017}, and hippocampal replay as sample-based planning \cite{pfeifferfoster2013}.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=0.25em, topsep=0.25em]
    \item A formal model of \emph{budgeted deliberation} with compute costs at the granularity of cognitive actions.
    \item The \emph{Budget--Performance Frontier} (BPF): a target curve for evaluation and a lens to analyze diminishing returns.
    \item Ten test-time algorithms with theory-backed properties: 
    Index-Guided Deliberation (IGD), Risk-Sensitive Monte Carlo Tree-of-Thought (RS-MCTT), 
    Abduction--Deduction--Refutation loops (ADR), Counterfactual Self-Consistency (CSC), 
    Decompose--Recompose via Subproblem Markets (DRSM), Branch-and-Bound with LLM Heuristics (BB-LLM),
    Annealed Population of Thoughts (APT), Deliberative External Memory with Ephemeral Indexing (DEMI),
    Probabilistic Self-Verification (PSV), and Dual-Process Gating (DPG).
    \item Approximation guarantees and risk bounds under natural assumptions (adaptive/submodular gains \cite{nemhauser1978,golovin2011adaptive}, concentration, admissible heuristics).
    \item A measurement protocol and a synthetic evaluation that instantiate BPF estimation, \emph{marginal value-of-compute} (MVC), reliability, effect-size reporting, and multiplicity control. We additionally delineate a concrete plan for real-world validation on GSM8K and MATH to complement the synthetic study.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Background, taxonomy, and prior-art differentiation}
CoT prompting \cite{wei2022cot} and self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency} increase reasoning coverage but typically allocate compute uniformly or via blind parallelism without principled stopping. Tree-of-Thoughts (ToT) \cite{yao2023tot} and multi-agent debate \cite{irving2018debate} introduce structure and critique but often rely on fixed schedules lacking compute-aware decision rules. Iterative self-improvement protocols (STaR \cite{zelikman2022star}, Self-Refine \cite{madaan2023selfrefine}, Reflexion \cite{shinn2023reflexion}) externalize feedback but again under-specify when to continue. Complementary lines in planning and control offer formal tools that our framework imports: bandit indices for multi-stream allocation \cite{gittins1979,whittle1988restless}, adaptive submodularity for diminishing returns \cite{golovin2011adaptive}, and risk-sensitive evaluation \cite{howard1972risk,ahmadi2012entropic}. Memory-augmented modeling (Memory Networks \cite{weston2015memnn}, DNC \cite{graves2016dnc}, RAG \cite{lewis2020rag}) expands context but raises amortized compute pricing questions. Prompting strategies such as least-to-most prompting \cite{zhou2022leasttomost} and synergistic reasoning-acting (ReAct) \cite{yao2022react}, as well as program-aided reasoning \cite{gao2023pal}, diversify how steps are generated, yet do not by themselves determine the \emph{quantity} and \emph{allocation} of test-time compute.

\paragraph{A comparative taxonomy of test-time compute policies.}
We categorize methods along three axes:
(i) \emph{Scheduling principle} (risk-neutral gains, risk-sensitive utility \cite{howard1972risk,ahmadi2012entropic}, index/EVC-ordered \cite{gittins1979});
(ii) \emph{Structure} (linear chains, trees/graphs, populations, markets);
(iii) \emph{Verification} (none, weak checks, strong specs).
CoT and self-consistency occupy the risk-neutral, uniform-allocation corner; ToT/Debate add structure without compute-aware control. Our algorithms occupy the structured, \emph{compute-aware} regime: IGD applies index-based scheduling; RS-MCTT adds risk-annealed search; ADR/PSV prioritize information/compliance checks; DRSM allocates via prices; APT maintains diverse populations; DEMI invests in ephemeral memory when EVC-positive. Kreps–Porteus preferences \cite{kreps1978temporal} motivate our depth-dependent risk attitudes in RS-MCTT, and energy-aware scheduling in cloud systems \cite{beloglazov2012cloud} parallels our compute- and cost-aware allocation under deployment constraints.

