\section{Computational complexity and system design}

We summarize per-decision-step asymptotics (with $b$ average branching factor, $d$ depth, $m$ threads, $M$ population size, $K$ checks, and $|M|$ memory slots). These are worst-case bounds ignoring constant-factor tokenization overheads:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=0.2em]
\item IGD: computing indices over $m$ threads requires posterior updates and a bounded-horizon lookahead; with amortized constant-time updates per step, $O(m)$. Risk-LCBs add $O(m)$ variance estimates. Memory $O(m)$ for thread state.
\item RS-MCTT: UCT-style selection/backup $O(b)$ per step with rollouts $O(d)$; total per node $O(b + d)$. Risk transforms use log-moment estimates, $O(1)$ per update. Memory proportional to visited nodes, $\tilde{O}(b^d)$ in worst case with substantial empirical pruning.
\item ADR: information-gain computation depends on check cost; for $K$ candidate checks, $O(K)$ scoring with cached posteriors.
\item CSC: constraint checks and message passing across $k$ chains: $O(kK)$ per round; majority voting $O(k)$.
\item DRSM: surplus computation across subproblems $|\mathcal{P}|$ is $O(|\mathcal{P}|)$; dual updates (e.g., ADMM) add $O(|\mathcal{P}|)$ per iteration \cite{boyd2011admm}.
\item BB-LLM: branch-and-bound is exponential in the worst case but prunes substantially with admissible bounds; per-node evaluation is dominated by heuristic computation and verifier calls.
\item APT: per epoch mutation/recombination $O(M)$; reweighting $O(M)$; resampling $O(M)$; diversity control adds negligible overhead.
\item DEMI: write selection with submodular gains is $O(|M|)$ greedy per step with $(1-1/e)$ approximation \cite{sviridenko2004}; retrieval/indexing cost depends on key-value store; locality-sensitive hashing typically $\tilde{O}(1)$ expected per query.
\item PSV: $O(K)$ likelihood updates; stopping via EVC-threshold is $O(1)$ once summaries are maintained.
\item DPG: gating decision $O(1)$ per example; escalation triggers the selected algorithm’s cost.
\end{itemize}
System design includes (i) a scheduler (EVC or indices), (ii) a search/planning module (e.g., RS-MCTT), (iii) verifiers and checks (PSV/BB-LLM), and (iv) ephemeral memory and retrieval (DEMI). Interfaces among modules pass scores, posteriors, and constraints rather than raw text, enabling calibrated and amortized decision rules. This mirrors energy- and latency-aware allocation in cloud systems \cite{beloglazov2012cloud} under cost/SLAs.

\section{Psychology and neuroscience links (expanded)}

The proposed algorithms mirror aspects of human deliberation and control, providing a resource-rational perspective on why compute-aware scheduling should work.

\subsection{Metareasoning and selective attention}
IGD and EVC formalize the idea that people prioritize computations by expected marginal value under constraints \cite{liedergriffiths2020}. Threads in IGD correspond to candidate hypotheses or problem decompositions; index ordering resembles selective attention and task prioritization in prefrontal control. The discount $\gamma$ captures temporal preference for near-term gains when working memory is limited.

\subsection{Risk, uncertainty, and commitment}
RS-MCTT implements depth-dependent risk sensitivity that becomes more averse near decision commitment. This mirrors human caution as uncertainty resolution approaches a final choice \cite{howard1972risk}. Kreps–Porteus preferences \cite{kreps1978temporal} clarify that when the timing of uncertainty resolution matters, non-expected-utility preferences can be rational; risk annealing in RS-MCTT is a normative analogue, discouraging brittle late-branch commitments.

\subsection{Hypothesis testing and falsification}
ADR’s \emph{abduction-deduction-refutation} loop aligns with Popperian falsification and Bayesian surprise: information gain measures how much an observation refines beliefs about correctness. Humans seek disconfirming evidence to avoid confirmation bias; ADR operationalizes this drive by scheduling high-yield refutation attempts first.

\subsection{Working memory and external notes}
DEMI’s ephemeral, instance-scoped memory parallels working memory and cognitive offloading: people take notes when the anticipated retrieval utility exceeds the cost of writing and searching. Submodular diminishing returns justify greedy note selection \cite{baddeley1992workingmemory, sviridenko2004}.

\subsection{Replay and sampling-based planning}
Hippocampal replay provides sample-based planning signals \cite{pfeifferfoster2013}. RS-MCTT rollouts and APT’s mutations are algorithmic analogues: they stochastically explore futures, evaluate counterfactuals, and bias sampling toward promising paths—consistent with replay gating by motivational salience and expected value.

\subsection{Fast and slow systems}
DPG bridges System~1 (fast heuristics) and System~2 (deliberation) \cite{kahneman2011,botvinick2019rlfastslow}: a calibrated gate escalates only when confidence falls below a budget-aware threshold. This yields bounded-risk performance with minimal expected compute, in line with human escalation from heuristics to analysis under uncertainty.

\section{Evaluation without experiments: what to measure (expanded)}

Beyond raw accuracy, compute-aware evaluation requires multi-faceted measurement.

\subsection{Frontier area and knees}
The \emph{frontier area} (FA) integrates performance over budgets:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{FA} \approx \sum_{i} \tfrac{1}{2}\,(P(B_i)+P(B_{i+1}))\,(B_{i+1}-B_i).
\end{equation}
FA rewards methods that perform well at both low and high budgets. A “knee” in $P(B)$ or MVC indicates the budget beyond which returns sharply diminish; this is crucial for deployment schedulers.

\subsection{Marginal value-of-compute and uncertainty}
MVC estimates $\Delta P/\Delta B$ using finite differences on the budget grid. We report 95\% CIs via a delta method: if $m_i$ denotes the mean at $B_i$ and $v_i$ its variance estimate, then
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\text{MVC}}(B_{i}) = \frac{m_i - m_{i-1}}{B_i - B_{i-1}}, \quad
\Var[\widehat{\text{MVC}}(B_{i})] \approx \frac{v_i + v_{i-1}}{(B_i - B_{i-1})^2}.
\end{equation}
Plotting MVC with ribbons reveals where additional compute pays off and where it does not.

\subsection{Reliability and calibration}
We quantify calibration using the Brier score and Expected Calibration Error (ECE). Let $\hat{p}$ be predicted correctness; then
\[
\text{Brier} = \E[(\hat{p}-\1\{\text{correct}\})^2], \qquad \text{ECE} = \sum_{b} \frac{|B_b|}{N} \left| \mathrm{acc}(B_b) - \mathrm{conf}(B_b)\right|.
\]
Risk-aware scheduling (RS-MCTT) and probabilistic checks (PSV) should reduce ECE at matched budgets by penalizing overconfident brittle reasoning and investing in verification when it increases posterior odds.

\subsection{Diversity and verifier yield}
APT/CSC aim to improve \emph{diversity efficiency}: quality gain per near-independent rationale. We track unique rationales (e.g., distinct decomposition trees) per unit compute and compute their marginal contribution. For PSV, we report \emph{verifier yield}: the gain in calibrated accuracy per unit verification compute, which informs whether to invest in more checks or more generation.

\subsection{Multiple testing and effect sizes}
We use Welch $t$-tests to compare methods at each budget, adjust across budgets using BH-FDR, and report Hedges’ $g$ to quantify magnitude. We exclude $B{=}0$ from tests to avoid degeneracy. Reporting both $p$-values and $g$ prevents misinterpretation and emphasizes practical significance.

\section{Discussion}

Our results emphasize that compute \emph{allocation} matters as much as compute \emph{quantity}. The BPF concavity highlights diminishing returns; thus, early-budget improvements (high MVC) are particularly valuable for latency-constrained or cost-sensitive settings. IGD’s index policy prioritizes the most promising reasoning thread given uncertainty and cost, while RS-MCTT’s risk annealing curbs brittle branches near decision time. These mechanisms, compatible with CoT/ToT generation, rationalize observed empirical gains.

Limitations remain. First, local EVC estimates and self-evaluations may be miscalibrated; explicit calibration (ECE/Brier) and verified checks help mitigate, but calibration itself requires careful design. Second, our synthetic setting abstracts away domain-specific constraints; real-world benchmarks (GSM8K, MATH, HumanEval) are needed to validate generality and inform practical hyperparameter schedules (e.g., index priors, risk temperatures). Third, population methods (APT) and markets (DRSM) can inherit biases from scoring functions; mitigation includes counterfactual checks (CSC), debiasing in scoring, and audit trails. Fourth, while we survey a broad method family, we deliberately evaluate the two core schedulers (IGD, RS-MCTT) in depth; the remaining modules are delivered as theory-backed blueprints with implementation guidance and will be evaluated in the proposed real-world plan.

Practically, we recommend: (i) deploying DPG to gate into structured deliberation only when needed; (ii) using IGD or RS-MCTT as the primary scheduler; (iii) incorporating PSV-style checks where weak verifiers exist; (iv) monitoring MVC and FA to decide budget schedules; and (v) instrumenting logs to estimate calibration and guide future tuning. As models evolve, the metareasoning layer can be reused, improving amortized deployment without retraining base LLMs.

\section{Related work}
Prompted reasoning traces such as CoT \cite{wei2022cot} and self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency} improve LLM problem-solving but lack formal compute allocation. 
Tree-of-thought search \cite{yao2023tot} and debate-style multi-agent prompting \cite{irving2018debate} add structure but often use fixed schedules. Our framework builds on metareasoning \cite{russellwefald1991,horvitz1989,liedergriffiths2020}, submodular optimization \cite{nemhauser1978,golovin2011adaptive}, risk-sensitive control \cite{howard1972risk,ahmadi2012entropic}, and bandit indices \cite{gittins1979,whittle1988restless}, and connects them to LLM inference and memory-augmented modeling \cite{weston2015memnn,graves2016dnc,lewis2020rag}, as well as prompt strategies like least-to-most \cite{zhou2022leasttomost}, ReAct \cite{yao2022react}, and PAL \cite{gao2023pal}.

Beyond these most closely related works, there is a broader literature on resource-bounded decision making and bounded rationality which our metareasoning perspective explicitly operationalizes. From the perspective of search, our RS-MCTT can be seen as a risk-sensitive variant of UCT \cite{kocsis2006uct} where the node value is transformed by an entropic risk functional; prior LLM search strategies that are risk-neutral may be brittle near commitment and lack explicit reliability controls. Our IGD connects to classical index policies; while Gittins indices are optimal only under certain assumptions, index heuristics are widely successful in practice, and we provide sufficient conditions and conservative LCB variants to mitigate misspecification. Finally, our ADR/PSV and DEMI components exploit information gain and submodularity, respectively, to schedule expensive verification and memory writes; these add compute-awareness to existing techniques (verification for math/program tasks, retrieval-augmented generation) by explicitly trading off expected gain against cost.

\section{Future work and open problems}
We highlight directions to catalyze a comprehensive program on compute-aware deliberation:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=0.2em]
\item \textbf{Calibration-first metareasoning:} Jointly learn EVC surrogates and calibration maps; study stability under distribution shift and adversarial prompting.
\item \textbf{Task- and domain-specific verifiers:} Extend PSV with learned verifiers and causal checks; study the compute–reliability Pareto frontier.
\item \textbf{Temporal preferences and risk:} Incorporate Kreps–Porteus-style preferences \cite{kreps1978temporal} to modulate when uncertainty should be resolved in RS-MCTT.
\item \textbf{Deployment constraints:} Couple allocation to latency, energy, and cost budgets \cite{beloglazov2012cloud}; integrate serverless scheduling and batching effects.
\item \textbf{Theoretical frontiers:} Tighten BPF concavity conditions; derive regret bounds for index policies under model misspecification; characterize adaptive submodularity in memory and verification settings.
\end{itemize}

\section{Reproducibility checklist}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=0.2em]
\item Code and configuration: the simulation script reads standardized TSV summaries; plotting functions produce BPF, MVC, and effect-size figures with CI ribbons. MVC ribbons are computed via a delta method from summary statistics.
\item Data: TSVs include budgets, means, sd, n, and CI bounds; ablation TSV uses explicit headers; an additional summary TSV provides effect sizes and $p$-values. We exclude $B{=}0$ from tests.
\item Determinism: Aggregation is deterministic; any randomized generation should fix seeds. Our plots derive from fixed TSVs.
\item Accounting: Compute budgets are matched across methods; pilot seeds (0--9) for tuning, evaluation seeds (10--59) for reporting.
\item Statistical testing: Welch tests and BH-FDR correction are computed from summary statistics; scripts report exact values and avoid degenerate $B{=}0$ tests.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

We introduced a unifying formalism for test-time compute scaling and ten algorithmic blueprints that transcend linear chains and naive parallelism. 
The central message is that \emph{where} and \emph{when} to spend computation can be decided using principled quantities (EVC, indices, risk measures, information gain, submodular gains), yielding anytime procedures with theoretical backing and cognitive plausibility.
Our synthetic evaluation illustrates how to estimate the BPF, MVC, and ablation effects with statistical rigor, and we articulated a concrete plan for real-world validation on GSM8K/MATH/HumanEval that will complement the current study.

